I have written a package called choroplethr <http://tech.truliablog.com/2014/02/14/choroplethr-version-1-1-0-released/> that facilitates creating choropleth maps in R.  At the core, the package uses ggplot2 to get state and county level maps as data.frames and then colors them in as described in Winston Chang's book "R Graphics Cookbook".

It also renders zip level data maps as scatterplots, getting the longitude and latitude coordinates from the package zipcode.  This is because map_data does not have a "zip" level map.  While I do not have extensive experience with maps, I do realize that there are a lot of problems <http://www.georeference.org/doc/zip_codes_are_not_areas.htm> with mapping zips.

What I would like to do is render the same boundaries that the Census uses for their ZIP Code Tabulated Areas (ZCTAs).  Those boundaries are available as a shapefile here <http://www.census.gov/cgi-bin/geo/shapefiles2013/main>.  However, this file is huge (501MB zipped), and my first naive attempt to plot it took forever.

My question is this: is there any way to convert this file into the same output that I would get with map_data?  Also, will the resulting data.frame be of a manageable size?

Thanks.

Ari

