Hi:

Here's a stab at it. I start with the temp data frame, use the
reshape2 package to melt it and then pass the melted data frame to
ggplot2.

library(reshape2)
library(ggplot2)

# Idea is to create a factor to distinguish the two responses ('variable');
# the values are strung together in a vector named 'value'
tmpM <- melt(temp, id = "myIndex")

ggplot(tmpM, aes(x = myIndex, y = value, color = variable)) +
    theme_bw() +
    geom_smooth(method = "glm", formula = y ~ x, fill = "gray85", size = 1)

I don't get exactly the same result you did. This way seems saner
because in your example graph, the upper confidence limits for the
success probability at small values of myIndex exceeded 1, which
shouldn't happen.

Dennis

