From u1e82ea932416652e at groups.google.com  Wed Feb 19 15:55:45 2014
From: u1e82ea932416652e at groups.google.com (Tom)
Date: Wed, 19 Feb 2014 15:55:45 +0000
Subject: Align grid.text to ggplot element?
Message-ID: <HK49rgz4jW0.mrXwVfNp-AgJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/HK49rgz4jW0/m/mrXwVfNp-AgJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I am attempting to create a chart that will automatically place labels outside of a chart relative to an indicator line (geom_vline, in this case), which is calculated from the supplied data. The final version of the graph will have additional annotation, and so the graph will actually be composed of several viewports, with the ggplot2 plot within one of these. I see four options for doing this:

1. Preferred approach: determine the position of the indicator line (geom_vline) in viewport npc, then draw the text in a separate viewport at the correct position. This is the approach illustrated in the sample code below. Ideally the annotation should be in a separate viewport, but I could place the annotation within the same viewport as the ggplot plot.
2. Draw the text using annotation_custom() and turn off clipping to place the text outside of the plot area. I have figured out how to do this, but would prefer to do the job without turning off clipping as it may cause problems with other annotations.
3. Place the annotation inside the plot area using annotate(), but the annotations will sometimes be drawn overtop some of the data, making the graph difficult to read.

My difficulty is that I cannot figure out how to determine the position of the indicator line (my.vertline, in the code below) relative to the viewport dimensions (npc or otherwise). Thanks in advance for suggestions.

Working sample below:

library(ggplot2)
library(grid)

#' Create the viewports
#' note that the height will not be constant; vp1 will be adjusted
#' based on content selected by the user
my.vp1 <- viewport(x = 0.5, y = 1, height = 0.2, just = c("centre","top"))
my.vp2 <- viewport(x = 0.5, y = 0, height = 0.8, just = c("centre","bottom"))

#' Create some sample data
my.df <- data.frame(index = seq(1:10), value = rnorm(10))
my.vertline <- 7         # will be calculated from the data and stored
my.rightmargin <- 4   # will be calculated from the data to ensure a large enough margin for certain annotations

#' Create the plot object
my.p <- ggplot(data = my.df) +
  theme(plot.margin = unit(c(1,my.rightmargin,2,1), "char")) +
  geom_point(aes(x = index, y = value)) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = my.vertline)
my.gt <http://my.gt> <- ggplot_gtable(ggplot_build(my.p))

#' New graph
grid.newpage()

pushViewport(my.vp1)
grid.text("Title Text")
popViewport()

pushViewport(my.vp2)
grid.draw(my.gt <http://my.gt>)
popViewport()

#' Now add the labels for the vertical line
#' Position "x" is what we want to calculate from the position of the vline,
#' which we will know.
pushViewport(my.vp1)
grid.text("left-side text", x = 0.61 - 0.01, y = 0, just = c("right","bottom"))
grid.text("right-side text", x = 0.61 + 0.01, y = 0, just = c("left","bottom"))
popViewport()

 <https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-45XRffzB3k8/UwTD-gzZj2I/AAAAAAAAFdo/9a030e-iy_4/s1600/example_plot.png>

From u9c9b5f9c6aaa025d at groups.google.com  Wed Feb 19 18:21:39 2014
From: u9c9b5f9c6aaa025d at groups.google.com (Doug Mitarotonda)
Date: Wed, 19 Feb 2014 18:21:39 +0000
Subject: Map, not set, axis tick labels
Message-ID: <DLjSNHjg-No.ucvXn0CEmU8J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/DLjSNHjg-No/m/ucvXn0CEmU8J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hey everyone,
Is it possible to map to axis tick labels, instead of setting them? My use case is that I am trying to focus attention on just a few discrete labels.

For example,

df <- data.frame(A = letters[1:3], B = 1:3, Is.focus = c(TRUE, FALSE, FALSE), stringsAsFactors = FALSE);
# This does a good job of highlighting the points in the plot...
p <- ggplot(df, aes(A, B)) + geom_point(aes(alpha = Is.focus), size = 6)
# ...but it would be nice to also map Is.focus to the tick labels as well, something like
p + theme(axis.text.x = element_text(color = "red", face = "bold"))
# where only `a` is red and bold, and `b` and `c` are the defaults

Would an option be to use `element_blank` and then `geom_text`? I am not sure how to get those to line up exactly where the tick marks should be, so any help there is appreciated.

Thanks,
Doug

From u0c39e60ddb38622b at groups.google.com  Wed Feb 19 21:30:25 2014
From: u0c39e60ddb38622b at groups.google.com (Dennis Murphy)
Date: Wed, 19 Feb 2014 21:30:25 +0000
Subject: Re: Map, not set, axis tick labels
Message-ID: <DLjSNHjg-No.Z9vlllpE7E0J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <DLjSNHjg-No.ucvXn0CEmU8J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <DLjSNHjg-No.ucvXn0CEmU8J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/DLjSNHjg-No/m/Z9vlllpE7E0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This isn't a mapping, but rather an ifelse() idiom, which works in
this case because Is.focus is logical:

p + theme(axis.text.x = element_text(color = ifelse(df$Is.focus,
"red", "black"),
                                     face = ifelse(df$Is.focus,
"bold", "plain")))

Dennis

